<h3 id="OckhSolu">2.4 Godâ€™s forebeliefs as â€œsoft factsâ€ about the past</h3>

<p>
The role of premise (2) in justifying the inference from (1) to (3)
has drawn more critical attention than any other move in the argument.
The most radical response, discussion of which will be postponed until
section 5, is to abandon the Principle of the Necessity of the Past
altogether. A less radical response is to clarify the conditions under
which the principle holds and argue that (1) does not satisfy these
conditions, thereby blocking the move to (3).</p>

<p>
This solution, which rejects premise (2) in its full generality, is
due to the fourteenth century philosopher William of Ockham, and was
revived in the contemporary literature by Marilyn Adams (1967).
Following Ockham, Adams argues that premise (2) applies only to the
past strictly speaking, or the â€œhardâ€ past. A
â€œsoftâ€ fact about the past is one that is in part about
the future. Examples of a soft fact about the past would include the
fact that it was true yesterday that George will send his
granddaughter a birthday card tomorrow, and the fact that I just
visited Paris for the last time. Adams argues that Godâ€™s past
beliefs about the future are not strictly past. Because they entail
facts about the future, they are in part about the future, and
consequently count as mere soft facts about the past.</p>

<p>
Adamsâ€™s argument was unsuccessful since, among other things, her
entailment criterion for being a soft fact had the consequence that no
fact is a hard fact: that Jack was sitting yesterday should be a hard
fact about the past, if anything is, but it entails that Jack
wonâ€™t sit tomorrow for the first time (Fischer 1989,
introduction). This led to a series of attempts to bolster Ockhamist
doubts about (2) by giving more refined definitions of a â€œhard
factâ€ and the type of necessity such facts are said to
haveâ€”what Ockham called â€œaccidental necessityâ€
(necessity <em>per accidens</em>). The 1980s in particular saw the
publication of multiple analyses of the hard-soft fact distinction
supportive of the Ockhamist thesis that Godâ€™s past beliefs about
the future should be regarded as soft facts about the past, including
Freddoso (1983), Hoffman and Rosenkrantz (1984), Kvanvig (1986),
Plantinga (1986), Wierenga (1989), and Craig (1990). Critiques of
these analyses appeared in Fischer (1983, 1985a, 1991), Hasker (1989),
Widerker (1990), Zagzebski (1991), and Pike (1993). One problem
hampering the Ockhamist case during this phase of the debate is that
the resulting formulations became so refined and elaborate, in an
effort to avoid possible counterexamples, that they risked becoming
detached from the simple intuition they were intended to capture
(Zagzebski 1991, 74â€“76).</p>

<p>
Plantinga (1986) suggests a way this debate could be short-circuited.
A successful Ockhamist response to theological fatalism neednâ€™t
await the definitive formulation of necessary and sufficient
conditions for soft facthood, because paradigm examples of soft
factsâ€”facts that are surely soft, if any facts are
softâ€”are enough for the job. Itâ€™s clear that proposition
<em>T</em>, for exampleâ€”that you will answer the phone tomorrow
at 9 amâ€”does not express a hard fact about the past. (It
doesnâ€™t express a fact about the past at all.) But if God is
necessarily existent and essentially omniscient, this fact about what
