avoid the dilemma posed in the dilemma defense (e.g., the
â€œbuffer casesâ€ proposed in Hunt 1999b and Pereboom 2000).
But if these or any of the other critiques of FSCs that have been
proposed are cogent, then PAP stands, and premise (9) is available to
justify step (10).</p>

<p>
Suppose on the other hand that FSCs <em>do</em> succeed in refuting
PAP. Then the argument fails, right? Maybe not. Itâ€™s true that
premise (9)â€”PAP in its full generalityâ€”would then be
false. But defenders of the argument might insist that (10)â€”the
argumentâ€™s fatalistic conclusionâ€”is nevertheless
warranted, on the grounds that PAP plays such a fundamental role in
our moral reasoning that it shouldnâ€™t be abandoned just because
itâ€™s possible to concoct a few recherchÃ© scenarios in
which it delivers the wrong result. FSCs constitute narrow exceptions
to PAP, which should continue to be honored except in cases that are
