proposed by Diodorus Cronus, whose argument is remarkably similar in
form to our basic argument for theological fatalism. The logical
fatalist argument parallels our basic argument as follows:</p>

<p>
<em>Argument for logical fatalism</em></p>

<p>
Let <em>S</em> = the proposition that there will be a sea battle
tomorrow.</p>

<dl class="sentag tag3em">
<dt>(1L)</dt>
<dd>Yesterday it was true that <em>S</em>. [assumption]</dd>
<dt>(2L)</dt>
<dd>If some proposition was true in the past, it is now-necessary that
it was true then. [Form of the Necessity of the Past]</dd>
<dt>(3L)</dt>
<dd>It is now-necessary that yesterday it was true that <em>S</em>.
[1, 2]</dd>
<dt>(4L)</dt>
<dd>Necessarily, if yesterday it was true that <em>S</em>, then now it
is true that <em>S</em>. [omnitemporality of truth]</dd>
<dt>(5L)</dt>
<dd>If it is now-necessary that <em>p</em> and if necessarily
(<em>p</em> then <em>q</em>), then it is now-necessary that q.
[Transfer of Necessity Principle]</dd>
<dt>(6L)</dt>
<dd>Therefore, it is now-necessary that now it is true that
<em>S</em>. [3L, 4L, 5L]</dd>
<dt>(7L)</dt>
<dd>If it is now-necessary that now it is true that <em>S</em>, no
alternative to the truth of <em>S</em> is now-possible. [definition of
â€œnecessaryâ€]</dd>
<dt>(8L)</dt>
<dd>So no alternative to the truth of <em>S</em> is now-possible [6L,
7L]</dd>
<dt>(9L)</dt>
<dd>If no alternative to the truth of a proposition about the future
is now-possible, then what the proposition is about will not be
brought about by free human choice. [Version of Principle of Alternate
Possibilities]</dd>
<dt>(10L)</dt>
<dd>Hence, the sea battle tomorrow will not be brought about by free
human choice. [8L, 9L]</dd>
</dl>

<p>
Unlike the argument for theological fatalism, the argument for logical
fatalism has few defenders, with the notable exception of Richard
Taylor (1963). One reason is that (2L) is less plausible than (2).
(1L) is a soft fact about the past, if anything is. Nevertheless, some
philosophers, like Susan Haack (1974) and William Lane Craig (1987),
have maintained that theological fatalism is just a gussied up version
of logical fatalism, and that the former is no more impressive than
the latter once one looks past the theological window-dressing. This
seems to be Merricksâ€™ (2009) position as well, since he holds
that theological fatalism fails for essentially the same reason as
logical fatalism. Warfield (1997) has argued for the equivalence of
the two forms of fatalism if God is necessarily existent and
essentially omniscient. Responses have been given by Hasker (1998) and
Brueckner (2000), and Warfield (2000) offers a rejoinder to both. Hunt
(2002b) links Warfieldâ€™s argument with Plantingaâ€™s (1986),
discussed in
 <a href="https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/free-will-foreknowledge/#ArisSolu">2.4</a>,
 inasmuch as both exploit the logical equivalence of propositions
about the contingent future with Godâ€™s believing those
propositions, and argues that they both fall prey to the same
reductio: the closure principles they invoke (closure of consistency
under logical equivalence for Warfield, closure of hard/soft facthood
under logical equivalence for Plantinga) would equally support the
compatibility of free will with divine determinism, an unacceptable
result for a libertarian. Peter Graham (2008) argues that the
consensus about consistency to which Warfield appeals emerged against
the backdrop of an assumption that there is no necessarily existent
being, and is therefore question-begging.</p>

<h2 id="BeyoFata">5. Beyond theological fatalism</h2>

<p>
In the opening paragraph of the paper that spawned the contemporary
debate, Pike observed that â€œthe claim that if God is omniscient,
no human action is voluntary . . . seems intuitively false. Surely,
given only a doctrine describing Godâ€™s <em>knowledge</em>,
nothing about the voluntary status of human actions will followâ€
(1965: 27). If Pikeâ€™s argument is successful, of course, what
seems intuitively false turns out to be surprisingly true. But
Pikeâ€™s comment suggests that the argument can also be taken
<em>aporetically,</em> as generating a puzzle to be solved.
Zenoâ€™s Achilles paradox is a classic aporetic problem, because
we are well within our epistemic rights in believing that
thereâ€™s <em>something</em> wrong with the argument without our
knowing, or even having any idea, <em>what</em> is wrong with it.
Craig maintains that this is just how matters stand when it comes to
the problem of theological fatalism:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
no matter how ingenious the argument, fatalism must be wrong. For it
posits a constraint upon human freedom which is altogether
unintelligible. The fatalist admits that our decisions and actions may
be causally freeâ€”indeed, they could be uttterly uncaused.
Nevertheless, such actions are said to be constrainedâ€”but by
what? Fate? What in the world is that? (1987: 68â€“69)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
The idea that an action otherwise satisfying the most exacting
conditions for free agency can turn out to be unfree just because God
knew in advance that it would occur seems nearly as absurd as that
Achilles canâ€™t pass a tortoise if the tortoise got a head start
(Hunt 2017a: 29â€“31).</p>

<p>
Todd (2022), taking issue with Craig, Hunt, and Fischer, in
particular, denies that the argumentâ€™s conclusion involves a
mysterious non-causal constraint on freedom. Since the argument
doesnâ€™t posit such a constraint, itâ€™s a mistake to
approach it aporetically; instead, the argument successfully
demonstrates that <em>something</em> makes the agent unfree, though
this something is most plausibly identified with the causal conditions
that must have obtained for God to foreknow the action in the first
place. This returns us to the question raised in
 <a href="https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/free-will-foreknowledge/#BoetSolu">2.2</a>
 about how future contingents could be known, even by God. Replies to
Todd may be found in Fischer (2025) and Wasserman (2025c), who
argue (among other things) that if â€œfreedom is the freedom to
add to the given pastâ€ (Carl Ginet 1990: 103), itâ€™s
unclear why constraints on freedom must be causal, as Todd
maintains.</p>

<p>
Thereâ€™s more at stake in the argument for theological fatalism
than the coherence of libertarian theism. A comparison with the
Achilles paradox might again be helpful. Thereâ€™s more at stake
in Zenoâ€™s argument than the fleetness of Achilles and the
torpidity of tortoises. If thatâ€™s all there was to it, the
discovery that Achilles was actually a quadraplegic, or that the
tortoises of ancient Greece were as fast as jack rabbits, would
resolve the puzzle. But that would simply exempt Achilles and/or the
tortoise from complicity in the problem; it would do nothing to
address the real issues presented by Zenoâ€™s argument. The
situation is arguably the same when it comes to the argument for
theological fatalism (Hunt 2017a). If the argument gets God wrong by
assuming that heâ€™s in time when he isnâ€™t, the problem
possibly goes away for God, once the mistake is corrected, but
itâ€™s easily reinstated by replacing God with Gud, an infallibly
omniscient being who exists in time. If the argument gets human beings
wrong by assuming that they have (libertarian) free will when they
donâ€™t, the problem can be reformulated in terms of Gudâ€™s
infallible beliefs about the future actions of Eleutherians, a race of
extraterrestrials stipulated to possess libertarian freedom. This is
to understand the argument for theological fatalism as a thought
experiment. Whether or not divine foreknowledge and libertarian
freedom are real, weâ€™re being asked, what if? Could libertarian
freedom really be incompatible with divine foreknowledge <em>for the
reasons given in the argument</em>? The answer to this question may
involve rethinking more than God and human freedom.</p>

<p>
At the heart of the argument is a transfer of temporal necessity from
a past event to a future event. It is assumed that there is a kind of
necessity that (1) the past has simply because it is past, where (2)
this temporal necessity is closed under entailment. Given this
necessity, if there is a proposition <em>P</em> that is wholly and
strictly about the past, and <em>P</em> entails a proposition
<em>Q</em> that is wholly and strictly about the future, then not only
is <em>P</em> temporally necessary, but so is <em>Q</em>. But then a
proposition that is wholly and strictly about the future will have a
necessity that the past has simply because it is past. This might be
puzzling even before foreknowledge and free will are brought into the
argument.</p>

<p>
It may be worth asking whether there is any such thing as the
necessity of the past at all. What do we mean when we say that the
past, the strict past, is <em>necessary?</em> When people say,
â€œThere is no use crying over spilled milk,â€ they
presumably mean that there is nothing anybody can do now about the
spilled milk; the spilling of the milk is outside the realm of our
causal control. But it is not at all clear that pastness <em>per
se</em> puts something outside the realm of our causal control.
Rather, it is pastness in conjunction with the metaphysical law that
causes must precede their effects. If we decided that effects can
precede their causes, itâ€™s quite possible that we would no
longer speak of the necessity of the past.</p>

<p>
So the necessity of the past may simply be the principle that past
events are outside the class of causable events. There is a temporal
asymmetry in causability because everything causable is in the future.
But some of the future is non-causable as well. Whether or not
determinism is true, there are some events in the future that are
causally necessary. If a future event <em>E</em> is necessary, it is
causable, and not-<em>E</em> is not causable. But if the necessity of
the past is the non-causability of the past, it would be odd to pick
out the class of propositions about the past as possessing an
allegedly distinct kind of necessity since some of the future has that
same kind of necessity.</p>

<p>
This leads to a deeper problem in the idea of the necessity of the
past. Zagzebski (2014) argues that the interpretation of the necessity
of the past as a purely temporal modality is confused. What people
generally mean by the necessity of the past is that the past is
causally closed, meaning the past is neither causable nor preventable.
The applicable transfer principle for the fatalistâ€™s argument
would then be</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
<strong>Transfer of Unpreventability Principle:</strong></p>

<p>
If <em>E</em> occurs and it is not now causable that <em>E</em> does
not occur, and necessarily (if <em>E</em> occurs, then <em>F</em>
occurs), then it is not now causable that <em>F</em> does not
occur.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
This principle is virtually identical to the transfer of
unpreventability principle proposed by Hugh Rice (2005), and is
similar to a strengthened form of van Inwagenâ€™s well-known rule
Î² introduced in
 <a href="https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/free-will-foreknowledge/#NecePastCausClosPast">2.6</a>.
 Here the necessity of the past is not a purely temporal modality, and
it is not a form of necessity. The categories of causability and
non-causability do not correspond to the standard modal categories of
the necessary, possible, and impossible. The attempt to assimilate the
causal categories to modal categories is a mistake.</p>

<p>
The dilemma of theological fatalism is broader than a problem about
free will (Zagzebski 1991, appendix). Itâ€™s also broader than a
problem about infallible foreknowledge. What foreknowledge contributes
to the dilemma is the possibility that <em>every</em> proposition
about the future is temporally necessary, because theyâ€™re
entailed by propositions about Godâ€™s past beliefs that are
temporally necessary. But the hypothesis of causal determinism has the
same consequence: that <em>every</em> proposition about the future is
temporally necessary, because theyâ€™re entailed by propositions
about past states of the universe that are temporally necessary.
Whether the entailment is grounded in the postulation of an infallibly
omniprescient being or the thesis of causal determinism, if the future
in its entirety is temporally necessary, it calls into question some
of our most fundamental assumptions about time, for example, that past
and future have a different ontological standing, that time has a
direction (an â€œarrow of timeâ€), and so on.</p>

<p>
Two books take up the problem of theological fatalism with special
attention to issues in the philosophy of time. De Florio and Frigerio
(2019) puts the metaphysics of time at the heart of the foreknowledge
dilemma and makes essential use of modern tense-logic as developed by
A. N. Prior. One solution they develop appeals to â€œperspectival
fragmentalism,â€ on which reality consists of incompatible but
equally real â€œperspectival factsâ€ that are relative to
time, place, or observer. Zagzebski (2024) confirms her skepticism
about the necessity of the past, arguing that it is impossible for
there to be a type of modality that has the following features:</p>

<dl class="sentag tag3em">
<dt>(a)</dt>
<dd>The past and future are asymmetrical in that the past qua past is
necessary with respect to this type of modality, whereas the future
qua future is contingent with respect to this type of modality.</dd>
<dt>(b)</dt>
<dd>There are propositions about the past that entail propositions
about the future.</dd>
<dt>(c)</dt>
<dd>Necessity with respect to this type of modality is closed under
entailment.</dd>
</dl>

<p>
She concludes that there is no modal arrow of time. The experience of
before and after is â€œnon-negotiable,â€ but it does not
require a temporal arrow.</p>

<p>
The problem of the alleged incompatibility of infallible foreknowledge
and free will is arguably a special case of a more general problem
about time and necessity. It may be necessary to achieve some clarity
on this latter problem before determining whether God and/or
libertarian freedom need to be rethought so that theyâ€™re not
complicit in the former problem.</p>

